; usbmsc.pi4 - USB Mass Storage, Bulk-Only Transport, over xhci.pi4.
;
; WHAT THIS IS FOR. The bench boots from a USB stick and always will.
; fat.pi4 has been able to parse FAT32 since the day it was written, and
; has been reading a fabricated image in memory because nothing could
; hand it real blocks. This file is the thing that hands it real blocks.
;
; The seam it plugs into is the filesystems' BLOCK-RANGE seam:
;
;     FsSetRangeReader(@MscReadBlocks)
;     FsSetRangeWriter(@MscWriteBlocks)
;
; (lba, count, *buf) -> 1 or 0. Until 2026-09-17 the seam was one block
; per call - (lba, *buf), SdReadBlock's shape - and this file answered it
; with one READ (10) per block, which held a SuperSpeed stick to 3 MB/s.
; See #MSC_XFER_BLOCKS for the measurement and forum 895.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT BULK-ONLY TRANSPORT ACTUALLY IS
; ======================================================================
;  USB Mass Storage Class Bulk-Only Transport, rev 1.0, and it is a
;  three-step conversation on two bulk endpoints:
;
;    1. the host writes a 31-byte Command Block Wrapper to bulk OUT
;    2. data moves, in whichever direction the CBW declared, if any
;    3. the device writes a 13-byte Command Status Wrapper to bulk IN
;
;  Every step is mandatory. Skipping the CSW read because the data
;  arrived leaves the device waiting to send it, and the NEXT command's
;  data phase then returns the previous command's status - which reads
;  as corruption rather than as a protocol error.
;
;  THE CBW, 31 bytes, all little-endian:
;
;     0   dCBWSignature        $43425355, the ASCII "USBC"
;     4   dCBWTag              echoed back in the CSW; used to detect
;                              a reply to a different command
;     8   dCBWDataTransferLength
;    12   bmCBWFlags           $80 = device to host, 0 = host to device
;    13   bCBWLUN              low four bits
;    14   bCBWCBLength         low five bits, 1..16
;    15   CBWCB[16]            the SCSI command itself
;
;  THE CSW, 13 bytes:
;
;     0   dCSWSignature        $53425355, the ASCII "USBS"
;     4   dCSWTag
;     8   dCSWDataResidue      bytes NOT transferred
;    12   bCSWStatus           0 pass, 1 fail, 2 phase error
;
;  A phase error is not a failed command. It means the two ends have
;  lost track of whose turn it is, and the only correct response is a
;  reset - which is why it gets its own error code here instead of
;  being folded into "command failed".
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE SCSI COMMANDS, AND WHY THESE SIX
; ======================================================================
;  The heading here said FOUR and listed four, while the file has always
;  defined six (#SCSI_* at :171-176). INQUIRY and WRITE (10) were both
;  missing from the prose. Corrected 2026-08-27 - a header that
;  undercounts what a file does is the same class of defect as one that
;  overstates what has been proven, and it is easier to fix than to
;  notice.
;  TEST UNIT READY ($00) - "are you there". Costs nothing and is the
;  only way to find out that a device needs its start-up time. A stick
;  that has just been powered will fail this several times before
;  passing, which is normal and NOT an error; MscInit retries.
;
;  REQUEST SENSE ($03) - why the last command failed. Mandatory after a
;  failure: many devices refuse everything else until the sense data is
;  collected. Skipping it turns one recoverable failure into a device
;  that never works again this session.
;
;  INQUIRY ($12) - vendor, product and revision strings. Not needed to
;  move a block, and worth its few lines anyway: it is what turns "a
;  medium answered" into "Samsung  Flash Drive FIT" on the console, and
;  a name is the difference between knowing which device replied and
;  assuming.
;
;  READ CAPACITY (10) ($25) - the last LBA and the block size, both
;  BIG-endian, which is the opposite of everything else here. SCSI is
;  big-endian and USB is little-endian and they meet in this file.
;
;  READ (10) ($28) - the original job.
;
;  WRITE (10) ($2A) - added later. MscWriteBlocks() is the counterpart of
;  MscReadBlocks() and has the same shape, so the filesystems take it
;  through FsSetRangeWriter(@MscWriteBlocks) exactly as they take the
;  reader.
;
;  THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "Writing is deliberately absent. A
;  bootloader reads." That was the design and it stopped being true, and
;  it is worth a line about WHY, because the reason is not scope creep.
;  The bench boots from a USB stick and there is no second medium: the
;  radio needs three firmware files on that stick and not one of them is
;  there to be replaced, so without a writer the stick has to come out
;  and go into another machine - the exact dependency the monitor's
;  `save` exists to remove. A bootloader that cannot write is a
;  bootloader that needs hands.
;
; ======================================================================
;  WHAT HAS RUN ON SILICON - and this file had no status section at all
; ======================================================================
;  It needed one. This is one of the most thoroughly exercised files in
;  the directory and a reader could not tell that from the header, which
;  is the same defect as a stale "nothing has run" banner with the sign
;  flipped: a file that says nothing about its own evidence gets weighed
;  by guesswork.
;
;  READING, 2026-08-26. Through a real VL805, a real xHCI controller and
;  a real Samsung Flash Drive FIT, at the monitor prompt:
;
;      mounted USB: Samsung  Flash Drive FIT   119 GB
;      medium    USB, 250626566 blocks of 512 bytes
;      volume    FAT32 on partition 1, cluster 4096 bytes
;
;  CONFIG.TXT came back at 813 bytes and matched the boot partition's
;  actual file; START4.ELF came back at 2,298,048 bytes.
;
;  WRITING, 2026-08-26. Three firmware files were created on that stick
;  through MscWriteBlock and each was read back off the volume into a
;  DIFFERENT address and CRC-checked on the board. The 609,309-byte
;  radio image later CRC'd to $B8B12CED - the value the host computed
;  before it was ever sent.
;
;  AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING AT EVERY BOOT: the firmware loads KERNEL8.IMG
;  off this stick, and everything the monitor loads afterwards comes
;  through here.
;
;  ---- THE BUG THAT COST THE MOST, because the shape recurs ----------
;
;  FALSE SHARING ON A CACHE LINE, and it wore three disguises first.
;  Every Bulk-Only command returned a status wrapper of thirteen ZERO
;  bytes while the controller correctly reported a thirteen-byte short
;  packet - so the device was answering and the answer was being
;  destroyed. It was blamed on SuperSpeed, then on short packets, then
;  on the retry loop supplying time. All three were wrong.
;
;  What settled it was asking for 64 bytes instead of 13: a real device
;  answers a short packet of exactly 13, and a fabricated completion
;  returns whatever was asked for. It returned 13.
;
;  msc_csw sat forty bytes into its cache line with the tail of msc_cbw
;  in the same line. msc_cbw is written by the CPU immediately before
;  each command and msc_csw by the DEVICE immediately after. ONE LINE,
;  TWO WRITERS - and no amount of cleaning or invalidating helps,
;  because both operate on the whole line. That is why the buffers below
;  are separate arrays rather than fields in a structure, and why the
;  comment under BUFFERS LIVE HERE says "cache-line aligned" rather than
;  merely "global".
;
;  ---- WHAT IS NOT PROVEN --------------------------------------------
;
;    * ONE DEVICE. Everything above is one Samsung stick on one port.
;      Start-up timing, LUN handling, sense-data recovery and phase
;      errors are all exercised only as far as that device provokes
;      them, and a phase error has never actually occurred.
;    * DURABILITY. A write that returns 1 has been ACCEPTED, not
;      necessarily committed to flash - there is no flush in the
;      Bulk-Only contract and none is issued here. fat.pi4's ordering
;      rules are only as good as this device's honesty about completion.
;    * NOTHING HAS BEEN INTERRUPTED MID-WRITE and inspected afterwards.
; ======================================================================
;  BUFFERS LIVE HERE, NOT ON THE STACK
; ======================================================================
;  Every buffer the controller touches is a global, because a bulk
;  transfer is DMA and the controller must be able to reach the memory
;  after this procedure's frame is gone. They are also cache-line
;  aligned by being separate arrays rather than fields in a structure.
;
;  Needs, in the main file: uart.pi4, timer.pi4, pcie.pi4, xhci.pi4.

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Constants
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MSC_CBW_BYTES = 31
#MSC_CSW_BYTES = 13
#MSC_CBW_SIG   = $43425355      ; "USBC"
#MSC_CSW_SIG   = $53425355      ; "USBS"
#MSC_DIR_IN    = $80            ; bmCBWFlags

#MSC_BLOCK_BYTES = 512          ; what fat.pi4's contract requires

#SCSI_TEST_UNIT_READY = $00
#SCSI_REQUEST_SENSE   = $03
#SCSI_INQUIRY         = $12
#SCSI_READ_CAPACITY10 = $25
#SCSI_READ10          = $28
#SCSI_WRITE10         = $2A
#SCSI_SYNC_CACHE10    = $35

; SENSE KEYS AND ADDITIONAL SENSE CODES this driver acts on. SPC-4 4.5.6.
; Sense data is the only thing that distinguishes "the command is wrong"
; from "ask me again" - both arrive as bCSWStatus 1 and nothing else about
; them differs.
#SCSI_SENSE_ILLEGAL_REQUEST = 5  ; the command or one of its fields is bad
#SCSI_SENSE_UNIT_ATTENTION  = 6  ; state changed under us; the command was
                                 ; NOT done, and is expected to be repeated
#SCSI_ASC_INVALID_OPCODE    = $20  ; there is no such command on this device
#SCSI_ASC_INVALID_CDB_FIELD = $24  ; the command exists, this field does not

; How many times a command is repeated while the device keeps answering
; UNIT ATTENTION. Three matches what the SCSI mid-layer above Linux's
; usb-storage does; a device that reports a new attention condition four
; times running has something else wrong with it.
#MSC_UA_TRIES = 3

; How many times TEST UNIT READY may fail before the device is declared
; absent. A stick coming out of reset commonly needs a handful.
#MSC_READY_TRIES = 40

#MSC_ERR_NONE       = 0
#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY  = -1   ; the bulk endpoints were never configured
#MSC_ERR_CBW        = -2   ; the CBW did not go out whole
#MSC_ERR_DATA       = -3   ; the data phase moved the wrong amount
#MSC_ERR_CSW        = -4   ; no CSW, or a short one
#MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG    = -5   ; the CSW signature was not "USBS"
#MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG    = -6   ; the CSW answered a different command
#MSC_ERR_FAILED     = -7   ; bCSWStatus 1 - the command failed
#MSC_ERR_PHASE      = -8   ; bCSWStatus 2 - the ends disagree
#MSC_ERR_NO_MEDIUM  = -9   ; TEST UNIT READY never passed
#MSC_ERR_CAPACITY   = -10  ; READ CAPACITY returned nonsense
#MSC_ERR_BLOCKSIZE  = -11  ; the medium is not 512-byte blocks
#MSC_ERR_ARG        = -12  ; a caller mistake
#MSC_ERR_RANGE      = -13  ; the LBA is past the end of the medium
#MSC_ERR_SHORT      = -14  ; a block command moved fewer bytes than asked
#MSC_ERR_RESET      = -15  ; reset recovery did not bring the device back
#MSC_ERR_CFG_READ   = -16  ; the configuration descriptor could not be read
#MSC_ERR_CFG_LONG   = -17  ; the configuration descriptor is longer than
                           ; this driver's buffer. NOT the same as "not a
                           ; stick", which is what it used to look like.
#MSC_ERR_NOT_BBB    = -18  ; a mass-storage device this driver's transport
                           ; cannot speak to - see MscUasAdvertised()

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  State. All DMA buffers are globals - see the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; ======================================================================
;  EVERY DMA BUFFER GETS ITS OWN CACHE LINE. THIS IS NOT TIDINESS.
; ======================================================================
;  These used to be three plain global arrays, and the status wrapper
;  came back as thirteen zero bytes every single time while the
;  controller correctly reported a thirteen-byte short packet. The
;  device was answering; the answer was being destroyed.
;
;  The buffers landed at whatever address the linker chose. msc_csw was
;  at $480C68 - forty bytes into its 64-byte cache line - and the tail
;  of msc_cbw was in that same line. msc_cbw is written by the CPU
;  immediately before every command; msc_csw is written by the DEVICE
;  immediately after. One cache line, two writers, and the CPU's copy
;  wins whenever it is written back.
;
;  That is false sharing, and on a non-coherent DMA path it is not a
;  performance problem, it is data loss. No amount of cleaning and
;  invalidating fixes it, because the granularity of both operations is
;  the LINE: there is no way to write back the CBW half without also
;  writing back the CPU's stale idea of the CSW half.
;
;  The tell was that control transfers worked perfectly through the same
;  driver. Those DMA into the xhci arena, which is bump-allocated with
;  64-byte alignment - so its buffers never share a line with anything.
;  The rule was already there and these three globals were simply
;  outside it.
;
;  SO: one backing array, and every buffer carved out of it on a 64-byte
;  boundary with a whole number of lines each. The extra #MSC_ALIGN
;  bytes are the slack the run-time alignment needs, because the
;  compiler's BSS is only guaranteed 16.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#MSC_ALIGN     = 64
#MSC_OFF_CBW   = 0            ; 64 bytes - one line
#MSC_OFF_CSW   = 64           ; 64 bytes - one line
#MSC_OFF_DATA  = 128          ; 512 bytes - eight lines
#MSC_OFF_BOUNCE = 640         ; 1 MiB - see msc_BlockIo
#MSC_POOL_USED = 1049216       ; 640 + 1048576
#MSC_POOL_BYTES = 1049280      ; #MSC_POOL_USED + #MSC_ALIGN of slack

; ======================================================================
;  HOW MANY BLOCKS ONE COMMAND CARRIES - and why this used to be one.
; ======================================================================
;  Until 2026-09-17 every READ (10) and WRITE (10) here asked for ONE
;  block. A block was a whole Bulk-Only conversation - command wrapper
;  out, 512 bytes, status wrapper in: three bulk transfers and three
;  completion events - and a 16 MiB load was 32,768 of them. Measured on
;  build 165 with the stick at super speed: 163 us per block at every
;  count, 3.07 MB/s, and no millisecond wait anywhere in the path (a 1 ms
;  wait per transfer would have shown as 3 ms per block). The time was
;  the round trips. Forum 895.
;
;  WRITES PAID FAR MORE than the round trips. On this stick a READ
;  between two single-block WRITEs adds about 4 ms to the write: the FAT32
;  writer saved 1 MiB in 0.86 s writing whole sectors and in 9.3 s when
;  every sector was read, modified and written. Many blocks per command
;  gives the device a run to program instead of a sector at a time.
;
;  1 MiB IS xh_Bulk's LIMIT FOR ONE TRANSFER, #XHCI_BULK_MAX: sixteen
;  64 KiB TRBs chained into one transfer descriptor. The block COUNT field
;  of READ (10) and WRITE (10) is 16 bits, so 2048 is well inside it. At
;  super speed the per-command cost is then a few hundred microseconds
;  against a megabyte of data, so the stick and the link set the rate,
;  not the conversation.
; ======================================================================
#MSC_XFER_BLOCKS = 2048
#MSC_XFER_BYTES  = 1048576    ; #MSC_XFER_BLOCKS * #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES

Global Dim msc_pool.a[#MSC_POOL_BYTES]
Global msc_base.i = 0           ; the aligned base, 0 until MscBuffers()

; Carve the pool. Idempotent, and called from everything that needs a
; buffer so no caller can forget.
Procedure MscBuffers()
  If msc_base <> 0
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  msc_base = (@msc_pool[0] + (#MSC_ALIGN - 1)) / #MSC_ALIGN
  msc_base = msc_base * #MSC_ALIGN
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Cbw()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_CBW
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Csw()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_CSW
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Data()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_DATA
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_Bounce()
  MscBuffers()
  ProcedureReturn msc_base + #MSC_OFF_BOUNCE
EndProcedure
Global msc_tag.i     = 0        ; increments per command
Global msc_err.i     = 0
Global msc_ready.i   = 0        ; 1 once capacity is known
Global msc_blocks.i  = 0        ; how many blocks the medium has
Global msc_blockSz.i = 0
Global msc_lastStatus.i = 0     ; the last bCSWStatus seen
Global msc_senseKey.i   = 0     ; from the last REQUEST SENSE
Global msc_senseAsc.i   = 0
Global msc_lastIn.i     = 0   ; bytes the last CSW read reported
Global msc_lastData.i   = 0   ; bytes the last data phase moved
Global msc_lastResidue.i = 0  ; dCSWDataResidue of the last status wrapper
Global msc_needReset.i  = 0   ; the last exchange left the two ends out of step
Global msc_uaRetries.i  = 0   ; UNIT ATTENTION retries since power-up (diagnostic)
Global msc_senseValid.i = 0   ; msc_senseKey/Asc describe THIS command's
                              ; refusal, not an older one
Global msc_resets.i     = 0   ; reset recoveries since power-up (diagnostic)
Global msc_iface.i      = 0   ; bInterfaceNumber of the mass-storage interface
Global msc_firstErr.i   = 0   ; why the FIRST TEST UNIT READY failed.
                              ; The fortieth failure is never the
                              ; interesting one; the first is.
Global Dim msc_vendor.a[16]     ; INQUIRY strings, NUL-terminated
Global Dim msc_product.a[24]

; THE MASS-STORAGE STEPS FOR THE BOOT TRAIL (forum 880). Each goes through
; the USB host's existing retained-trace hook, so this file adds no variable
; and no dependency: with no hook installed xh_Trace records the code and
; does nothing else. The codes, as the boot trail decodes them:
;   $0Bxx  the command wrapper for SCSI opcode xx is about to go out
;   $0Cxx  a status wrapper came back with status xx (0 good, 1 failed, 2 phase)
;   $0Dxx  the command failed here with this file's error xx
;   $0Exx  a bulk transfer did not complete; xx is the host's completion code
;   $11xx  REQUEST SENSE said sense key xx      $12xx  and ASC xx
;   $13xx  READ(10) of a block whose number ends in byte xx
Procedure.i msc_Fail(code.i)
  xh_Trace($0D00 | (code & $FF))
  msc_err = code
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; DIAGNOSTIC ACCESSORS. The wrappers are the protocol, and when the two
; ends fall out of step the only way to see why is to look at the bytes
; that actually arrived rather than at a decoded field.
Procedure.i MscCswAddr() : ProcedureReturn msc_Csw() : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscCbwAddr() : ProcedureReturn msc_Cbw() : EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscError()
  ProcedureReturn msc_err
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Little-endian and big-endian helpers.
;
;  Spelled out per byte rather than done with a 32-bit poke, because the
;  two orders live side by side in this file - USB is little-endian and
;  SCSI is big-endian - and a reader has to be able to see which is
;  which at the point of use.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure msc_PutLe32(*p, off.i, v.i)
  PokeA(*p + off + 0, v & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 1, (v >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 2, (v >> 16) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 3, (v >> 24) & $FF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_GetLe32(*p, off.i)
  ProcedureReturn PeekA(*p + off + 0) | (PeekA(*p + off + 1) << 8) | (PeekA(*p + off + 2) << 16) | (PeekA(*p + off + 3) << 24)
EndProcedure

Procedure msc_PutBe32(*p, off.i, v.i)
  PokeA(*p + off + 0, (v >> 24) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 1, (v >> 16) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 2, (v >> 8) & $FF)
  PokeA(*p + off + 3, v & $FF)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i msc_GetBe32(*p, off.i)
  ProcedureReturn (PeekA(*p + off + 0) << 24) | (PeekA(*p + off + 1) << 16) | (PeekA(*p + off + 2) << 8) | PeekA(*p + off + 3)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  msc_Command - one whole CBW / data / CSW exchange.
;
;  *cdb is the SCSI command, cdbLen its length. dir is #MSC_DIR_IN or 0.
;  *buf/len describe the data phase and may be 0/0.
;
;  Returns 1 on a command that both transferred and reported success.
;  Anything else sets msc_err and returns 0 - including a command that
;  moved its data perfectly and then reported failure, because that is
;  what bCSWStatus is for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i msc_Exchange(*cdb, cdbLen.i, dir.i, *buf, len.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  Define sig.i
  Define tag.i

  If XhciBulkReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  If cdbLen < 1 Or cdbLen > 16
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf

  msc_tag = msc_tag + 1
  msc_lastData = 0
  msc_lastResidue = 0

  ; ---- the CBW ------------------------------------------------------
  i = 0
  While i < #MSC_CBW_BYTES
    PokeA(msc_Cbw() + i, 0)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  msc_PutLe32(msc_Cbw(), 0, #MSC_CBW_SIG)
  msc_PutLe32(msc_Cbw(), 4, msc_tag)
  msc_PutLe32(msc_Cbw(), 8, len)
  PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 12, dir & $80)
  PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 13, 0)              ; LUN 0
  PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 14, cdbLen & $1F)
  i = 0
  While i < cdbLen
    PokeA(msc_Cbw() + 15 + i, PeekA(*cdb + i))
    i = i + 1
  Wend

  xh_Trace($0B00 | (PeekA(*cdb) & $FF))
  n = XhciBulkOut(msc_Cbw(), #MSC_CBW_BYTES)
  If n <> #MSC_CBW_BYTES
    xh_Trace($0E00 | (XhciLastComp() & $FF))
    msc_needReset = 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CBW)
  EndIf

  ; ---- the data phase, if any ---------------------------------------
  If len > 0 And *buf <> 0
    If (dir & #MSC_DIR_IN) <> 0
      n = XhciBulkIn(*buf, len)
    Else
      n = XhciBulkOut(*buf, len)
    EndIf
    ; A SHORT data phase is not automatically an error - the residue in
    ; the CSW is the authority - but a NEGATIVE one is, and so is a
    ; stall. Let the CSW below decide about a short read; refuse only
    ; on a transfer that did not happen.
    If n < 0
      xh_Trace($0E00 | (XhciLastComp() & $FF))
      ; A STALLED DATA PHASE IS AN ANSWER, NOT A LOST CONVERSATION. The
      ; device refused the data (BOT 6.7.2 / 6.7.3) and still has a status
      ; wrapper to hand over. The controller's halt is already cleared
      ; (xh_Bulk); clear the device's, then read the CSW as usual - Linux
      ; usb_stor_Bulk_transport does exactly this. Returning here instead
      ; left that CSW queued, and the NEXT command's reply arrived one
      ; exchange late.
      If XhciLastError() <> #XHCI_ERR_STALL
        msc_needReset = 1
        ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_DATA)
      EndIf
      If (dir & #MSC_DIR_IN) <> 0
        i = XhciClearEndpointHalt(XhciBulkEpIn())
      Else
        i = XhciClearEndpointHalt(XhciBulkEpOut())
      EndIf
      If i = 0
        msc_needReset = 1
        ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_DATA)
      EndIf
      n = 0
    EndIf
    msc_lastData = n
  EndIf

  ; ---- the CSW ------------------------------------------------------
  ; ALWAYS read it, even after a failed data phase. The device has one
  ; queued and will hand it to the next command's data phase otherwise.
  ; ASK FOR MORE THAN A STATUS WRAPPER IS, ON PURPOSE.
  ;
  ; A CSW is 13 bytes. Requesting exactly 13 makes "the device sent 13"
  ; and "the controller invented 13 because that is what we asked for"
  ; indistinguishable - and the buffer was arriving as zeros with the
  ; transfer reporting success, so telling those apart is the whole
  ; question. Request 64 and a real device answers with a SHORT PACKET
  ; of exactly 13; anything reporting 64 moved is not the device
  ; talking.
  n = XhciBulkIn(msc_Csw(), 64)
  If n < 0 And XhciLastError() = #XHCI_ERR_STALL
    ; A stalled status phase: clear the device's halt and ask once more
    ; (BOT 6.7.2; Linux transport.c "Attempting to get CSW (2nd try)").
    If XhciClearEndpointHalt(XhciBulkEpIn()) <> 0
      n = XhciBulkIn(msc_Csw(), 64)
    EndIf
  EndIf
  msc_lastIn = n
  If n < #MSC_CSW_BYTES
    xh_Trace($0E00 | (XhciLastComp() & $FF))
    msc_needReset = 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CSW)
  EndIf
  sig = msc_GetLe32(msc_Csw(), 0)
  If sig <> #MSC_CSW_SIG
    msc_needReset = 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG)
  EndIf
  tag = msc_GetLe32(msc_Csw(), 4)
  If tag <> msc_tag
    ; The reply belongs to an earlier command. Continuing would attach
    ; this status to the wrong request.
    msc_needReset = 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG)
  EndIf

  msc_lastResidue = msc_GetLe32(msc_Csw(), 8)
  msc_lastStatus = PeekA(msc_Csw() + 12)
  xh_Trace($0C00 | (msc_lastStatus & $FF))
  If msc_lastStatus = 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_FAILED)
  EndIf
  If msc_lastStatus = 2
    msc_needReset = 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_PHASE)
  EndIf

  msc_err = #MSC_ERR_NONE
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  msc_ResetRecovery - Bulk-Only Transport 5.3.4, reset recovery (forum 917).
;
;  After a command the two ends no longer agree about - a phase error, a
;  status wrapper that is missing or answers another tag, a transfer that
;  failed or timed out - the stick is not asked anything else until:
;
;    1. Bulk-Only Mass Storage Reset, the class request, to the interface;
;    2. CLEAR_FEATURE(ENDPOINT_HALT) on bulk IN, then bulk OUT;
;    3. the controller's two bulk endpoints stopped and configured again,
;       which cancels whatever was left on their rings and resets their
;       sequence numbers to match what step 2 did at the device.
;
;  The order and the requests are Linux's usb_stor_reset_common
;  (transport.c:1347-1392) and Circle's Reset (usbmassdevice.cpp:696-733).
;  The 100 ms settle after the class reset is Circle's; Linux allows up to
;  six seconds only while waiting for a disconnect to interrupt it.
;
;  Without this, one failed transfer left build 173's stick answering
;  nothing until the board restarted.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i msc_ResetRecovery()
  Define t0.i
  msc_resets = msc_resets + 1
  xh_Trace($0D00 | ((#MSC_ERR_RESET) & $FF))
  If XhciMscClassReset(msc_iface) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; Accounted in the xHCI wait ledger, with every other settle in the USB
  ; path, so that "where did the boot go" has ONE table to read.
  t0 = XhciWaitMark()
  delay(100)
  XhciAccountWait(#XHCI_W_MSCRESET, t0)
  If XhciClearEndpointHalt(XhciBulkEpIn()) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If XhciClearEndpointHalt(XhciBulkEpOut()) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn XhciBulkReconfigure()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  msc_Sense - REQUEST SENSE, and the only place the answer is decoded.
;
;  Lives above msc_Command because msc_Command calls it: a device that
;  answers CHECK CONDITION has told us nothing except that it said no, and
;  the sense data is the whole of the difference between "that command does
;  not exist" and "ask me again". MscRequestSense below is this same
;  procedure under its public name.
;
;  IT GOES OUT AS A PLAIN EXCHANGE, not through msc_Command, and that is
;  deliberate rather than a shortcut. msc_Command calls this procedure, so
;  the reverse would be a cycle in the call graph - which this driver may
;  not have (tools/usb_frame_safety_check.py refuses one, and the compiler
;  is entitled to refuse it too). Nothing is lost: if the sense exchange
;  itself leaves the two ends out of step it sets msc_needReset like any
;  other, and msc_Command's recovery picks that up on the way out.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i msc_Sense()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_REQUEST_SENSE
  cdb[4] = 18
  msc_senseValid = 0
  If msc_Exchange(@cdb[0], 6, #MSC_DIR_IN, msc_Data(), 18) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; Sense key is the low nibble of byte 2; the additional sense code is
  ; byte 12. Together they say things like "not ready, becoming ready",
  ; which is the normal answer from a stick that is still spinning up.
  msc_senseKey = PeekA(msc_Data() + 2) & $0F
  msc_senseAsc = PeekA(msc_Data() + 12)
  xh_Trace($1100 | msc_senseKey)
  xh_Trace($1200 | (msc_senseAsc & $FF))
  msc_senseValid = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  msc_Command - one whole CBW / data / CSW exchange, recovered once.
;
;  Returns 1 on a command that both transferred and reported success, or 0
;  with msc_err set. A command that left the two ends out of step gets
;  reset recovery and ONE retry - what Circle does (MAX_TRIES) and what the
;  SCSI layer above Linux's usb-storage does - so a single lost exchange
;  costs a retry instead of the stick.
;
;  AND A DEVICE THAT SAYS "ASK ME AGAIN" IS ASKED AGAIN. bCSWStatus 1 is an
;  answer, not a broken exchange, and it used to be handed straight back to
;  the caller. But a Bulk-Only reset - the recovery above - is exactly the
;  event that makes the next command answer CHECK CONDITION with UNIT
;  ATTENTION, key 6: a state change the initiator is being told about, with
;  the command NOT performed. So one hiccup cost the recovery, then spent
;  the retry on the attention condition, and the real command never ran.
;  On a write that is the worst shape there is - part of a file old, part
;  new, no error anywhere near the window this lane started from.
;
;  MscInit's TEST UNIT READY loop has always tolerated this; block I/O
;  could not, because nothing under it ever issued REQUEST SENSE. Now the
;  sense data is collected on every status 1, and only key 6 is repeated -
;  at most #MSC_UA_TRIES times. Every other key is the device's answer and
;  is returned as it is.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i msc_Command(*cdb, cdbLen.i, dir.i, *buf, len.i)
  Define tries.i
  Define ua.i
  Define ok.i
  Define keep.i
  Define again.i
  tries = 0
  ua = 0
  keep = #MSC_ERR_NONE
  again = 1
  While again = 1
    again = 0
    msc_needReset = 0
    msc_senseValid = 0
    ok = msc_Exchange(*cdb, cdbLen, dir, *buf, len)
    If ok = 1
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    keep = msc_err
    ; The device answered and said no. Ask it why, and repeat the command
    ; if the answer was "the state changed, that one did not happen".
    If msc_needReset = 0
      If keep = #MSC_ERR_FAILED
        If ua < #MSC_UA_TRIES
          If msc_Sense() = 1
            If msc_senseKey = #SCSI_SENSE_UNIT_ATTENTION
              ua = ua + 1
              msc_uaRetries = msc_uaRetries + 1
              again = 1
            EndIf
          EndIf
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
    ; The two ends out of step - from this command or from the sense that
    ; followed it - is reset recovery, once, then the command again.
    If again = 0
      If msc_needReset <> 0
        If tries = 0
          If msc_ResetRecovery() = 0
            ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_RESET)
          EndIf
          tries = 1
          again = 1
        EndIf
      EndIf
    EndIf
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(keep)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscRequestSense - collect the sense data after a failure.
;
;  Called by MscInit's retry loop. Many devices will refuse every
;  command until this is done, so a driver that skips it after the first
;  failure never recovers.
;
;  This is msc_Sense above under its public name, and it is a call rather
;  than a copy on purpose: two procedures that decode sense data are two
;  places to get the nibble wrong.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscRequestSense()
  ProcedureReturn msc_Sense()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscLastIn() : ProcedureReturn msc_lastIn : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscFirstError() : ProcedureReturn msc_firstErr : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscSenseKey()  : ProcedureReturn msc_senseKey : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscSenseAsc()  : ProcedureReturn msc_senseAsc : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscTestUnitReady - one poll. 1 if the unit answered ready.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscTestUnitReady()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_TEST_UNIT_READY
  ProcedureReturn msc_Command(@cdb[0], 6, 0, 0, 0)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscInquiry - who is this. Fills the vendor and product strings.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscInquiry()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_INQUIRY
  cdb[4] = 36
  If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 6, #MSC_DIR_IN, msc_Data(), 36) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; Bytes 8..15 are the vendor and 16..31 the product, space-padded
  ; ASCII with no terminator. Copied out and terminated here so callers
  ; can print them without knowing that.
  i = 0
  While i < 8
    msc_vendor[i] = PeekA(msc_Data() + 8 + i)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  msc_vendor[8] = 0
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    msc_product[i] = PeekA(msc_Data() + 16 + i)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  msc_product[16] = 0
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscVendor()  : ProcedureReturn @msc_vendor[0]  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscProduct() : ProcedureReturn @msc_product[0] : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscReadCapacity - the last LBA and the block size, BOTH BIG-ENDIAN.
;
;  READ CAPACITY(10) returns the address of the LAST block, not the
;  count. Off by one here means the last block of the medium is
;  unreadable, which on a FAT volume is usually in the middle of the
;  data area and presents as one corrupt file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscReadCapacity()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  Define lastLba.i
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_READ_CAPACITY10
  If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 10, #MSC_DIR_IN, msc_Data(), 8) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  lastLba     = msc_GetBe32(msc_Data(), 0) & $FFFFFFFF
  msc_blockSz = msc_GetBe32(msc_Data(), 4) & $FFFFFFFF
  If msc_blockSz <= 0 Or lastLba <= 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CAPACITY)
  EndIf
  ; fat.pi4's contract is 512-byte blocks. A 4096-byte medium is a real
  ; thing and would need the filesystem layer to know; refusing is the
  ; honest answer rather than reading a quarter of every cluster.
  If msc_blockSz <> #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_BLOCKSIZE)
  EndIf
  msc_blocks = lastLba + 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscBlockCount() : ProcedureReturn msc_blocks  : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscBlockSize()  : ProcedureReturn msc_blockSz : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscReady()      : ProcedureReturn msc_ready   : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscInit - bring the logical unit up. The bulk endpoints must already
;  be configured by XhciConfigureBulk.
;
;  Returns 1, or 0 with MscError() set.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscInit()
  Define tries.i
  Define t0.i

  msc_ready = 0
  msc_blocks = 0
  msc_blockSz = 0
  If XhciBulkReady() = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf

  ; TEST UNIT READY, repeatedly. The first few failures are expected on
  ; a device that has just been reset and are NOT reported as errors -
  ; only running out of patience is. Sense data is collected after each
  ; failure because some devices will not proceed until it is.
  ; LET THE DEVICE SETTLE AFTER SET_CONFIGURATION.
  ;
  ; The very first Bulk-Only exchange after Configure Endpoint came back
  ; with a status wrapper that was not USBS, every time, on two different
  ; code paths - and every exchange after it worked. A probe that
  ; happened to issue one throwaway command first "worked", which is a
  ; horrible thing to depend on and was the clue: the retry loop was
  ; quietly supplying time, not recovering from anything.
  ;
  ; USB 2.0 section 9.2.6.4 allows a device up to 5 ms of recovery after
  ; a SET_CONFIGURATION before it must respond. Nothing above waits, so
  ; the wait is here, once, before the first command. Generous rather
  ; than minimal: this happens exactly once per device and the cost of
  ; being wrong is a device that never works.
  t0 = XhciWaitMark()
  delay(50)
  XhciAccountWait(#XHCI_W_MSCCONF, t0)

  tries = 0
  msc_firstErr = 0
  While tries < #MSC_READY_TRIES
    If MscTestUnitReady() = 1
      Break
    EndIf
    ; KEEP THE FIRST REASON. Reporting only "never became ready" after
    ; forty attempts hides whether the very first one failed in the
    ; command wrapper, the data phase or the status phase - and those
    ; are three unrelated faults that a retry loop turns into one
    ; useless sentence.
    If tries = 0
      msc_firstErr = msc_err
    EndIf
    MscRequestSense()
    t0 = XhciWaitMark()
    delay(50)
    XhciAccountWait(#XHCI_W_MSCREADY, t0)
    tries = tries + 1
  Wend
  If tries >= #MSC_READY_TRIES
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NO_MEDIUM)
  EndIf

  If MscInquiry() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If MscReadCapacity() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  msc_ready = 1
  msc_err = #MSC_ERR_NONE
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; A scratch for one WHOLE configuration descriptor. Its own, so
; MscSetupCurrent does not borrow the command data buffer that a
; concurrent read might be using.
;
; IT WAS 192 BYTES AND THE DRIVER ONLY EVER ASKED FOR 64, which was the
; defect: a device whose descriptor did not fit in 64 bytes was refused
; with no error at all, and the commonest reason for a longer one is a
; SECOND ALTERNATE SETTING - which is exactly what a USB 3 stick that
; also speaks UAS has. The whole modern half of the market read as "not
; mass storage" and said nothing. 512 is the control transfer's own
; bounce size, so the buffer can no longer be the smaller limit, and a
; descriptor that still does not fit is refused BY NAME.
#MSC_CFG_BYTES = 512
Global Dim msc_cfg.a[#MSC_CFG_BYTES]
; What the walk over that descriptor found out, kept so `usb storage`
; can say it rather than the driver deciding alone and staying quiet.
Global msc_uas.i = 0        ; a UAS alternate setting is advertised
Global msc_cfgLen.i = 0     ; wTotalLength, as the device reported it
Global msc_ifProto.i = -1   ; the mass-storage interface protocol seen
Global msc_slot.i = 0           ; the xHCI slot the stick was set up on

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscSetupCurrent - is the CURRENTLY ADDRESSED xHCI device a USB mass-
;  storage stick, and if so, make it usable. Returns 1 if this slot is
;  now a working block device, 0 if it is not mass storage (leave it for
;  the caller to try as HID) or could not be brought up.
;
;  THIS IS WHAT LETS THE MOUSE, THE KEYBOARD AND THE STICK SHARE ONE
;  CONTROLLER. The enumeration walk addresses every device once; for
;  each it offers the slot here, and a stick is configured on the spot
;  without a second controller bring-up. Bulk transfers already target
;  xh_mscSlot (set by XhciConfigureBulk) and HID control transfers target
;  the per-poll xh_slot, so the two never collide once both are up.
;
;  NO PRINTING - it runs inside the walk and a device that is simply not
;  a stick is not news.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscSetupCurrent()
  Define got.i
  Define total.i
  Define alt.i
  Define bbb.i
  Define cfgVal.i
  Define off.i
  Define len.i
  Define typ.i
  Define cls.i
  Define sub.i
  Define prot.i
  Define epOut.i
  Define epIn.i
  Define mpsOut.i
  Define mpsIn.i
  Define burstOut.i
  Define burstIn.i
  Define lastEp.i

  ; A stick is already set up. If it is this same slot, done; a second
  ; stick on another slot is not something one bulk pair can serve.
  If XhciBulkReady() <> 0
    If XhciBulkReady() = XhciSlot()
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; NINE BYTES FIRST, BECAUSE wTotalLength IS INSIDE THE DESCRIPTOR and
  ; there is no other way to learn how much to ask for - USB 2.0 table
  ; 9-10. Asking for a fixed 64 and refusing anything longer is what this
  ; used to do, and it is the same mistake as reading a length field and
  ; then ignoring it.
  msc_uas = 0
  msc_cfgLen = 0
  msc_ifProto = -1
  ; ONE READER FOR THIS, IN THE USB CORE. The two-step read lived here for
  ; a few hours and then the device listing needed the same one; two copies
  ; of a length-obeying read is two places to get the bounds wrong. It is
  ; in Anvil/Bus/usb_core.pbi because it is USB and not xHCI, and a
  ; chip file cannot be included by another board's build at all.
  total = UsbGetConfiguration(@msc_cfg[0], #MSC_CFG_BYTES)
  If total < 9
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CFG_READ)
  EndIf
  msc_cfgLen = total
  If total > #MSC_CFG_BYTES
    ; LOUDLY. A device this driver cannot hold the descriptor of is a
    ; limit in this driver, and saying so is the whole difference between
    ; a bug report and a shrug.
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CFG_LONG)
  EndIf
  got = UsbConfigurationHeld()
  If got < total
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_CFG_READ)
  EndIf
  cfgVal = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + 5)

  cls = 0
  sub = 0
  prot = 0
  epOut = 0
  epIn = 0
  mpsOut = 0
  mpsIn = 0
  burstOut = 0
  burstIn = 0
  lastEp = 0
  bbb = 0
  off = 0
  While off < total
    len = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off)
    typ = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 1)
    If len = 0
      Break
    EndIf
    If typ = 4
      ; AN INTERFACE DESCRIPTOR, AND ITS ALTERNATE SETTING MATTERS. USB
      ; 2.0 table 9-12: byte 2 is bInterfaceNumber and byte 3 is
      ; bAlternateSetting. A UAS stick publishes the SAME interface
      ; number twice - alternate 0 is Bulk-Only (protocol $50) and
      ; alternate 1 is UAS (protocol $62) with four endpoints of its
      ; own. Walking the list without reading byte 3 takes the LAST
      ; interface seen, which is the UAS one, and then refuses the
      ; device for not being Bulk-Only while its Bulk-Only interface
      ; was sitting there all along.
      alt  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 3)
      cls  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 5)
      sub  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 6)
      prot = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 7)
      If cls = 8
        ; $62 is USB Attached SCSI - a FASTER transport this driver does
        ; not speak. Recording that it was offered is not idle: a stick
        ; running at Bulk-Only speed when it advertised UAS is a fact
        ; worth seeing at the prompt rather than guessing at from a
        ; datasheet.
        If prot = $62
          msc_uas = 1
        EndIf
        If alt = 0 And sub = 6 And prot = $50
          msc_iface = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2)
          msc_ifProto = prot
          bbb = 1
        Else
          bbb = 0
        EndIf
      Else
        bbb = 0
      EndIf
      lastEp = 0
    ElseIf typ = 5 And bbb <> 0
      lastEp = 0
      If (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 3) & 3) = 2
        If (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2) & $80) <> 0
          epIn  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2)
          mpsIn = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 4) | (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 5) << 8)
          lastEp = 2
        Else
          epOut  = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2)
          mpsOut = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 4) | (PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 5) << 8)
          lastEp = 1
        EndIf
      EndIf
    ElseIf typ = $30 And len >= 3 And bbb <> 0
      ; SUPERSPEED ENDPOINT COMPANION (USB 3.2 section 9.6.7). It follows
      ; the endpoint it describes; bMaxBurst at byte 2 is how many packets
      ; the endpoint may send before an acknowledgement, less one.
      If lastEp = 1
        burstOut = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2) & $0F
      ElseIf lastEp = 2
        burstIn = PeekA(@msc_cfg[0] + off + 2) & $0F
      EndIf
      lastEp = 0
    EndIf
    off = off + len
  Wend

  ; SCSI transparent (6) over Bulk-Only ($50), on ALTERNATE SETTING 0 -
  ; which is what msc_ifProto records, and the only shape MscInit's
  ; command set can speak to. A device that has no such interface is
  ; refused by name, and MscUasAdvertised() says whether the reason was
  ; that it offered a transport this driver does not have.
  If msc_ifProto <> $50
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_BBB)
  EndIf
  If epOut = 0 Or epIn = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_BBB)
  EndIf
  If XhciSetConfiguration(cfgVal) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If XhciConfigureBulk(epOut, mpsOut, burstOut, epIn, mpsIn, burstIn) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If MscInit() = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  msc_slot = XhciSlot()
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  msc_Copy - n bytes, eight at a time when both ends allow it.
;
;  The bounce below moves up to 1 MiB per command, and a byte loop over
;  that is measurable beside a USB transfer in a way a 512-byte one was
;  not. Overlap is never asked for: the bounce buffer is this file's own.
;
;  WORDS ONLY WHEN BOTH ADDRESSES ARE 8-BYTE ALIGNED. With the MMU off -
;  which is how this monitor boots, and what `cache off` returns it to -
;  all memory is Device memory, and an unaligned 64-bit load or store
;  there is an alignment fault on the part, not a slower access
;  (tools/a64/a64_interp.py, align_guard). A buffer one byte off a word
;  is exactly what gets bounced, so the word path must not assume it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure msc_Copy(*dst, *src, n.i)
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  If ((*dst | *src) & 7) = 0
    While i + 8 <= n
      PokeI(*dst + i, PeekI(*src + i))
      i = i + 8
    Wend
  EndIf
  While i < n
    PokeA(*dst + i, PeekA(*src + i))
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  msc_BlockIo - READ (10) or WRITE (10) of `count` blocks at `lba`, in
;  commands of up to #MSC_XFER_BLOCKS blocks each.
;
;  THE DATA PHASE GOES STRAIGHT TO THE CALLER'S MEMORY WHEN THAT IS SAFE,
;  and through the pool's aligned bounce buffer when it is not. Safe means
;  two things, both checked per command:
;
;    * the controller can reach it (PcieDmaOk), and
;    * it starts on a 64-byte cache line. The length is a whole number of
;      512-byte blocks, so it then ENDS on one too, and every line the
;      transfer touches belongs to this transfer alone. That is the whole
;      of the false-sharing rule at the top of this file: the status
;      wrapper came back as zeros because its line was shared with bytes
;      the CPU wrote. A buffer made of whole lines cannot share one.
;
;  Anything else - an odd address, a stack buffer, memory above the DMA
;  window - is bounced, so no caller can get this wrong.
;
;  A COMMAND THAT MOVED LESS THAN IT ASKED FOR IS A FAILURE HERE, whatever
;  its status byte says. A short block read would leave stale bytes in the
;  caller's buffer looking fresh, and a short write would leave the medium
;  holding a sector nobody wrote. Both the data phase's own count and the
;  status wrapper's residue must say every byte moved.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i msc_BlockIo(opcode.i, lba.i, count.i, *buf)
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  Define done.i
  Define n.i
  Define len.i
  Define dir.i
  Define *at
  Define *io

  If *buf = 0 Or count < 1
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_ARG)
  EndIf
  If msc_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  ; Compared as "count > blocks - lba" so a huge count cannot wrap the sum.
  If lba < 0 Or lba >= msc_blocks Or count > msc_blocks - lba
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_RANGE)
  EndIf
  dir = 0
  If opcode = #SCSI_READ10
    dir = #MSC_DIR_IN
  EndIf

  done = 0
  While done < count
    n = count - done
    If n > #MSC_XFER_BLOCKS
      n = #MSC_XFER_BLOCKS
    EndIf
    len = n * #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES
    *at = *buf + done * #MSC_BLOCK_BYTES
    *io = *at
    If (*at & (#MSC_ALIGN - 1)) <> 0 Or PcieDmaOk(*at, len) = 0
      *io = msc_Bounce()
      If opcode = #SCSI_WRITE10
        msc_Copy(*io, *at, len)
      EndIf
    EndIf

    i = 0
    While i < 16
      cdb[i] = 0
      i = i + 1
    Wend
    cdb[0] = opcode
    ; The LBA is big-endian at bytes 2..5 and the block COUNT is
    ; big-endian at bytes 7..8.
    msc_PutBe32(@cdb[0], 2, lba + done)
    cdb[7] = (n >> 8) & $FF
    cdb[8] = n & $FF
    If opcode = #SCSI_READ10
      xh_Trace($1300 | ((lba + done) & $FF))
    EndIf

    If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 10, dir, *io, len) = 0
      ProcedureReturn 0
    EndIf
    If msc_lastData <> len Or msc_lastResidue <> 0
      ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_SHORT)
    EndIf
    If opcode = #SCSI_READ10 And *io <> *at
      msc_Copy(*at, *io, len)
    EndIf
    done = done + n
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscReadBlocks / MscWriteBlocks - THE BLOCK-RANGE SEAM.
;
;      Procedure.i Reader(lba.i, count.i, *buf)   -> 1 or 0
;      Procedure.i Writer(lba.i, count.i, *buf)   -> 1 or 0
;
;  Pass @MscReadBlocks to FsSetRangeReader and @MscWriteBlocks to
;  FsSetRangeWriter and both filesystems read and write the USB stick a
;  RUN of blocks per call. The seam used to be one block per call, and
;  every layer above it was shaped by that; see #MSC_XFER_BLOCKS.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscReadBlocks(lba.i, count.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn msc_BlockIo(#SCSI_READ10, lba, count, *buf)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscWriteBlocks(lba.i, count.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn msc_BlockIo(#SCSI_WRITE10, lba, count, *buf)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscFlush - SYNCHRONIZE CACHE (10): the device commits what it accepted.
;
;  THE FILESYSTEMS' FLUSH SEAM (FsSetBlockFlusher). Bulk-Only has no flush
;  of its own and a WRITE (10) that returned good status has been ACCEPTED,
;  not necessarily committed - see WHAT IS NOT PROVEN in the header. With
;  whole megabytes going out per command the device's write cache holds
;  far more at once than a sector did, so the flush the filesystems issue
;  before they clear the volume's dirty mark is now asked of the device.
;
;  LBA 0 and a block count of 0 mean "the whole medium" (SBC-3 5.22). A
;  device that does not implement the command answers CHECK CONDITION with
;  ILLEGAL REQUEST; that is not a failed flush but an absent one, and it is
;  reported as success, because refusing to finish every save on such a
;  stick would make it unwritable.
;
;  THERE ARE TWO WAYS A DEVICE SAYS "no such command" and only one of them
;  was accepted here. ASC $20, INVALID COMMAND OPERATION CODE, is the
;  common one. ASC $24, INVALID FIELD IN CDB, is what a device answers when
;  it has the opcode but not the shape of the request - the IMMED bit, or a
;  block count it will not take - and several USB bridges answer a whole-
;  medium SYNCHRONIZE CACHE that way. Both mean the same thing to a caller:
;  this device will not be flushed by this command, ever, and nothing is
;  pending because nothing was attempted.
;
;  NOTHING ELSE IS ACCEPTED. A key that is not ILLEGAL REQUEST, or an ASC
;  outside those two, is a flush that failed, and the volume's dirty mark
;  must stay set. UNIT ATTENTION (key 6) is not seen here at all: it means
;  "ask me again", and msc_Command has already done so up to #MSC_UA_TRIES
;  times before this procedure gets its answer - so key 6 arriving here is
;  a device that has announced a new state change four times running, which
;  is a real failure and is reported as one.
;
;  The sense data is the sense data msc_Command collected for THIS refusal.
;  Asking for it a second time would get NO SENSE, because the first
;  REQUEST SENSE clears the condition - which is exactly how a driver ends
;  up deciding on an empty answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscFlush()
  Dim cdb.a[16]
  Define i.i
  If msc_ready = 0
    ProcedureReturn msc_Fail(#MSC_ERR_NOT_READY)
  EndIf
  i = 0
  While i < 16
    cdb[i] = 0
    i = i + 1
  Wend
  cdb[0] = #SCSI_SYNC_CACHE10
  If msc_Command(@cdb[0], 10, 0, 0, 0) = 1
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If msc_err <> #MSC_ERR_FAILED
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If msc_senseValid = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If msc_senseKey = #SCSI_SENSE_ILLEGAL_REQUEST
    If msc_senseAsc = #SCSI_ASC_INVALID_OPCODE
      msc_err = #MSC_ERR_NONE
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    If msc_senseAsc = #SCSI_ASC_INVALID_CDB_FIELD
      msc_err = #MSC_ERR_NONE
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
  EndIf
  msc_err = #MSC_ERR_FAILED
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscReadBlock / MscWriteBlock - one block. What the raw `usb read`
;  service slot (SvcUsbReadBlock, frozen ABI) and the installer example
;  call; the same command path as a run of one.
;
;  WRITING WAS ONCE DELIBERATELY ABSENT, because a bootloader reads. That
;  stopped being true the moment Anvil became the boot image: updating
;  Anvil means writing to the medium it boots from, and the alternative is
;  a second machine and a pulled stick every time. It is still not a
;  general write path - there is no cluster allocation here; this writes
;  blocks something else has already decided belong to a file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscReadBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn msc_BlockIo(#SCSI_READ10, lba, 1, *buf)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i MscWriteBlock(lba.i, *buf)
  ProcedureReturn msc_BlockIo(#SCSI_WRITE10, lba, 1, *buf)
EndProcedure

; Did this device advertise USB Attached SCSI as well as Bulk-Only? 1 if
; a class-8 interface with protocol $62 appeared anywhere in its
; configuration descriptor. It is a fact about the DEVICE, not about what
; this driver did with it: a stick that says UAS and is being driven
; Bulk-Only is leaving its queueing behind, and nobody could tell before.
Procedure.i MscUasAdvertised() : ProcedureReturn msc_uas : EndProcedure
; wTotalLength of that descriptor, and the mass-storage interface's
; bInterfaceProtocol as actually used (-1 if none was).
Procedure.i MscConfigLength() : ProcedureReturn msc_cfgLen : EndProcedure
Procedure.i MscInterfaceProtocol() : ProcedureReturn msc_ifProto : EndProcedure
; bInterfaceNumber of the interface this driver claimed, so a listing can
; say which one is claimed rather than assuming it is interface 0.
Procedure.i MscInterface() : ProcedureReturn msc_iface : EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  MscErrorText - one sentence per code, naming the STAGE.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i MscErrorText()
  Select msc_err
    Case #MSC_ERR_NONE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: ok"
    Case #MSC_ERR_NOT_READY
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the bulk endpoints are not configured - XhciConfigureBulk first"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CBW
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the 31-byte command wrapper did not go out whole"
    Case #MSC_ERR_DATA
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the data phase failed"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CSW
      ProcedureReturn "msc: no status wrapper came back, or it was short"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the status wrapper's signature was not USBS - the two ends are out of step"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the status wrapper answered a different command"
    Case #MSC_ERR_FAILED
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the device reported the command failed"
    Case #MSC_ERR_PHASE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: phase error - the device and host disagree about whose turn it is, and only a reset fixes that"
    Case #MSC_ERR_NO_MEDIUM
      ; Name the FIRST failure, not the last. See MscFirstError.
      Select msc_firstErr
        Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_SIG
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and the first status wrapper was not USBS - the bulk endpoints are not carrying data"
        Case #MSC_ERR_CSW
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and no status wrapper came back at all"
        Case #MSC_ERR_CBW
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and the command wrapper would not go out"
        Case #MSC_ERR_CSW_TAG
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready, and the status wrappers are answering earlier commands - the two ends are one reply apart"
        Case #MSC_ERR_FAILED
          ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit reported not-ready every time - it is talking, but it has no medium"
      EndSelect
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the unit never became ready"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CAPACITY
      ProcedureReturn "msc: READ CAPACITY returned a size that cannot be right"
    Case #MSC_ERR_BLOCKSIZE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the medium does not use 512-byte blocks, which the filesystem layer requires"
    Case #MSC_ERR_ARG
      ProcedureReturn "msc: bad argument"
    Case #MSC_ERR_RESET
      ProcedureReturn "msc: a command failed, and the Bulk-Only reset that should have brought the stick back failed too - reseat the stick or restart the board"
    Case #MSC_ERR_SHORT
      ProcedureReturn "msc: a block command moved fewer bytes than it asked for, so the blocks were not all read or written"
    Case #MSC_ERR_RANGE
      ProcedureReturn "msc: that block is past the end of the medium"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CFG_READ
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the device would not hand over its configuration descriptor, so nothing about its interfaces is known - reseat it and look at usb tree"
    Case #MSC_ERR_CFG_LONG
      ProcedureReturn "msc: the device's configuration descriptor is longer than this driver's buffer, so it was not read whole and no interface was trusted - this is a limit here, not a fault in the device"
    Case #MSC_ERR_NOT_BBB
      ProcedureReturn "msc: this is a mass-storage device, but not one this driver's Bulk-Only transport can drive - type usb storage to see what it advertises"
  EndSelect
  ProcedureReturn "msc: unknown error"
EndProcedure
